1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,038
Ava: If a training job has to move to another chip, how much of the software stack has to move with it?
Ava：如果训练任务要迁移到另一种芯片，软件栈需要跟着迁移多少？

2
00:00:06,038 --> 00:00:11,549
Brian: That's the practical question behind PyTorch Conference China twenty twenty-six.
Brian：这正是 PyTorch Conference China 2026 背后的实际问题。

3
00:00:11,549 --> 00:00:16,996
The conference looked at how an open AI stack can span different hardware.
大会探讨了开放的 AI 技术栈如何支持不同硬件。

4
00:00:16,996 --> 00:00:21,690
Ava: So this wasn't just a conference about new PyTorch features?
Ava：所以，这不只是一场介绍 PyTorch 新功能的大会？

5
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:22,103
Brian: Right.
Brian：对。

6
00:00:22,103 --> 00:00:30,920
It brought the community together in Shanghai on September eighth and ninth, after two related events on September seventh.
两场相关活动于9月7日举行后，社区又于9月8日至9日在上海相聚。

7
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:36,550
Ava: And the theme was Open Source for the AI Era. What does that mean in practice?
Ava：主题是“Open Source for the AI Era”，也就是“AI 时代的开源”。具体是什么意思？

8
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:39,824
Brian: It means looking beyond the framework alone.
Brian：就是不能只看框架本身。

9
00:00:39,824 --> 00:00:47,316
The discussions covered models, training, inference, hardware, cloud infrastructure, and agents.
讨论涵盖模型、训练、推理、硬件、云基础设施和智能体。

10
00:00:47,316 --> 00:00:52,130
Ava: That's a lot of ground. Is there one idea that connects it?
Ava：范围真广。有什么共同的主线吗？

11
00:00:52,130 --> 00:01:00,352
Brian: The article calls it one open source AI stack: software and hardware layers that different communities build together.
Brian：文章称之为一个开源 AI 技术栈：由不同社区共同构建的软件和硬件层。

12
00:01:00,352 --> 00:01:05,406
Ava: Let's slow down. When you say stack, what sits on top of what?
Ava：先说清楚，技术栈里各层是怎么排列的？

13
00:01:05,406 --> 00:01:08,126
Brian: At the top are applications and agents.
Brian：最上层是应用和智能体。

14
00:01:08,126 --> 00:01:16,148
Beneath them are tools for building and delivering intelligence, then tools for running and scaling workloads.
下面是构建和交付智能能力的工具，再下面是运行和扩展工作负载的工具。

15
00:01:16,148 --> 00:01:18,058
Ava: And below those?
Ava：再往下呢？

16
00:01:18,058 --> 00:01:22,829
Brian: Infrastructure and isolation, then heterogeneous compute.
Brian：是基础设施与隔离层，然后是异构计算。

17
00:01:22,829 --> 00:01:32,136
Heterogeneous just means different kinds of processors, including CPUs, GPUs, NPUs, and other accelerators.
异构就是指不同类型的处理器，包括 CPU、GPU、NPU 和其他加速器。

18
00:01:32,136 --> 00:01:38,366
Ava: So a developer working on an agent still depends on choices several layers below it.
Ava：所以开发智能体的人，也会受到下面好几层技术选择的影响。

19
00:01:38,366 --> 00:01:42,381
Brian: Exactly. Imagine changing the engine in a vehicle.
Brian：没错。想象一下给车换发动机。

20
00:01:42,381 --> 00:01:47,068
You'd want the controls above it to keep making sense.
你会希望上面的操控方式仍然好用。

21
00:01:47,068 --> 00:01:53,922
Ava: That's the appeal. Does the article say every model already runs on every chip?
Ava：这就是吸引人的地方。文章说所有模型现在都能在所有芯片上运行吗？

22
00:01:53,922 --> 00:02:00,151
Brian: No. Any Model, Any Chip, Any Cloud, Any Agent is the Foundation's framing.
Brian：没有。“Any Model, Any Chip, Any Cloud, Any Agent”是基金会提出的愿景。

23
00:02:00,151 --> 00:02:05,552
The article also describes the adaptation work still needed.
文章也谈到了仍需完成的适配工作。

24
00:02:05,552 --> 00:02:09,790
Ava: What projects did the Foundation put in those middle layers?
Ava：基金会把哪些项目放在中间几层？

25
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:15,188
Brian: It placed PyTorch, vLLM, and Ray in the building and delivering layer.
Brian：构建和交付层包括 PyTorch、vLLM 和 Ray。

26
00:02:15,188 --> 00:02:20,628
vLLM comes up elsewhere in the program in connection with serving.
议程其他部分也提到了 vLLM，涉及模型服务。

27
00:02:20,628 --> 00:02:23,258
Ava: And running workloads at scale?
Ava：那大规模运行工作负载这一层呢？

28
00:02:23,258 --> 00:02:29,951
Brian: That layer included Kubernetes, KServe, Kueue, OpenTelemetry, and llm-d.
Brian：这一层包括 Kubernetes、KServe、Kueue、OpenTelemetry 和 llm-d。

29
00:02:29,951 --> 00:02:36,064
The article presents them as examples within the larger stack.
文章把它们作为整个技术栈中的例子。

30
00:02:36,064 --> 00:02:40,014
Ava: Then OpenStack and Kata Containers are below that?
Ava：再往下就是 OpenStack 和 Kata Containers？

31
00:02:40,014 --> 00:02:42,788
Brian: Yes, in infrastructure and isolation.
Brian：对，它们属于基础设施与隔离层。

32
00:02:42,788 --> 00:02:48,236
The key point is that these layers meet at real workload boundaries.
关键是，这些层会在实际工作负载的边界上相接。

33
00:02:48,236 --> 00:02:52,666
Ava: So who takes responsibility when a boundary doesn't work?
Ava：如果边界衔接出了问题，谁来负责？

34
00:02:52,666 --> 00:02:55,107
Brian: That's where collaboration matters.
Brian：这正是协作重要的地方。

35
00:02:55,107 --> 00:03:03,408
The Foundation says no single organization builds the whole system; its members work across different parts of it.
基金会表示，没有哪家机构能独自构建整个系统；成员们分别在不同环节开展工作。

36
00:03:03,408 --> 00:03:07,982
Ava: The article also announces new members. Who joined?
Ava：文章还宣布了新成员。谁加入了？

37
00:03:07,982 --> 00:03:12,077
Brian: Alibaba Cloud and Cambricon joined as Platinum Members.
Brian：阿里云和寒武纪成为白金会员。

38
00:03:12,077 --> 00:03:14,608
Ant Group joined as a Gold Member.
蚂蚁集团成为金牌会员。

39
00:03:14,608 --> 00:03:19,276
The announcement happened in Shanghai on September eighth.
这一消息于9月8日在上海宣布。

40
00:03:19,276 --> 00:03:24,474
Ava: Do their backgrounds show why the Foundation talks about a whole stack?
Ava：他们各自的背景能说明基金会为什么强调完整技术栈吗？

41
00:03:24,474 --> 00:03:30,684
Brian: They do. Alibaba Cloud's work spans cloud infrastructure and the Qwen model family.
Brian：能。阿里云的业务涵盖云基础设施和 Qwen 模型系列。

42
00:03:30,684 --> 00:03:36,272
Ant Group brings production AI experience at financial-services scale.
蚂蚁集团带来了金融服务规模的 AI 生产实践经验。

43
00:03:36,272 --> 00:03:39,526
Ava: And Cambricon is the hardware side?
Ava：寒武纪代表硬件这一端？

44
00:03:39,526 --> 00:03:42,371
Brian: Yes. It develops MLU accelerators.
Brian：对，它开发 MLU 加速器。

45
00:03:42,371 --> 00:03:49,164
The article says its PyTorch contributions follow an Upstream First approach.
文章说，它对 PyTorch 的贡献遵循 Upstream First（上游优先）原则。

46
00:03:49,164 --> 00:03:53,426
Ava: Upstream First sounds important, but what did it contribute?
Ava：上游优先听起来很重要，它具体贡献了什么？

47
00:03:53,426 --> 00:04:03,832
Brian: The article lists work across torch.compile, Eager Operators, Device Runtime, Distributed Computing, AMP, Dataloader, and Profiler.
Brian：文章列出了 torch.compile、Eager Operators、Device Runtime、Distributed Computing、AMP、Dataloader 和 Profiler 等方面的工作。

48
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:06,918
Ava: That's more than a single device hook.
Ava：这远不止接入一个设备那么简单。

49
00:04:06,918 --> 00:04:11,213
Brian: Right. It touches several places a workload passes through.
Brian：对，工作负载经过的多个环节都涉及了。

50
00:04:11,213 --> 00:04:16,004
The article doesn't spell out the individual changes, though.
不过，文章没有逐一说明具体改动。

51
00:04:16,004 --> 00:04:22,834
Ava: Before we get to hardware integration, how large is the China-based contributor community?
Ava：先不谈硬件集成，中国的贡献者社区有多大？

52
00:04:22,834 --> 00:04:31,896
Brian: The article reports more than one hundred China-based developers contributing to PyTorch across more than forty affiliated organizations.
Brian：文章称，来自中国 40 多家相关机构的 100 多名开发者参与了 PyTorch 贡献。

53
00:04:31,896 --> 00:04:38,246
Ava: I also saw a figure above two hundred and fifty. Is that the same count?
Ava：我还看到一个超过 250 的数字，统计的是同一批人吗？

54
00:04:38,246 --> 00:04:51,028
Brian: No. That figure covers organizations across China contributing to PyTorch Foundation projects, including PyTorch, Ray, DeepSpeed, Safetensors, and vLLM.
Brian：不是。这张图展示了中国各地为 PyTorch 基金会项目作出贡献的组织，涉及 PyTorch、Ray、DeepSpeed、Safetensors 和 vLLM。

55
00:04:51,028 --> 00:04:53,238
Ava: That's a useful distinction.
Ava：这个区别很重要。

56
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:59,034
One count is PyTorch developers; the other spans Foundation projects.
一个统计的是 PyTorch 开发者，另一个涵盖基金会的多个项目。

57
00:04:59,034 --> 00:05:05,840
Brian: Exactly. Put them together carelessly, and you'd give listeners the wrong picture.
Brian：没错。把两个数字混为一谈，会让听众产生误解。

58
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:11,614
Ava: Now, back to chips. What's making hardware support so difficult?
Ava：回到芯片。硬件支持为什么这么难？

59
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:19,412
Brian: The article points to hardware heterogeneity, high adaptation costs, and a lack of unified standards.
Brian：文章指出，原因包括硬件种类繁多、适配成本高，以及缺乏统一标准。

60
00:05:19,412 --> 00:05:23,700
Each new chip can create repeated integration work.
每增加一种芯片，都可能要重复做一遍集成工作。

61
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:27,002
Ava: Repeated where? Just in operators?
Ava：哪些地方要重复？只有算子吗？

62
00:05:27,002 --> 00:05:28,654
Brian: Operators are one part.
Brian：算子只是其中一部分。

63
00:05:28,654 --> 00:05:36,904
The September seventh discussions also named compilers, training and inference frameworks, and communications.
9 月 7 日的讨论还提到了编译器、训练和推理框架，以及通信。

64
00:05:36,904 --> 00:05:43,206
Ava: So it's like adding a machine to a lab and discovering every tool needs a new connector.
Ava：就像实验室添了一台机器，却发现每件工具都得换个接口。

65
00:05:43,206 --> 00:05:45,347
Brian: Yes. That's a helpful picture.
Brian：对，这个比喻很贴切。

66
00:05:45,347 --> 00:05:54,260
The FlagOS-hosted event discussed open system software for diverse hardware and collaboration across chip architectures.
由 FlagOS 主办的活动讨论了适配多种硬件的开放系统软件，以及跨芯片架构协作。

67
00:05:54,260 --> 00:05:57,802
Ava: There was a Huawei-hosted event too, wasn't there?
Ava：华为也主办了一场活动，对吧？

68
00:05:57,802 --> 00:05:58,665
Brian: There was.
Brian：对。

69
00:05:58,665 --> 00:06:10,320
It focused on PyTorch workloads across GPUs, NPUs, and XPUs, including migration, operators, compiler work, and performance tuning.
活动聚焦 PyTorch 工作负载如何在 GPU、NPU 和 XPU 上运行，包括迁移、算子、编译器开发和性能调优。

70
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:14,054
Ava: Can you make the Foundation's response more concrete?
Ava：能具体说说基金会是怎么应对的吗？

71
00:06:14,054 --> 00:06:22,708
Brian: The PyTorch Technical Advisory Council has an Accelerator Integration Working Group, co-chaired by Huawei and Intel.
Brian：PyTorch 技术咨询委员会设有加速器集成工作组，由华为和英特尔共同牵头。

72
00:06:22,708 --> 00:06:25,506
Ava: What does that group actually deliver?
Ava：这个工作组具体提供什么？

73
00:06:25,506 --> 00:06:37,088
Brian: It provides standardized hardware onboarding guidelines, cross-repo CI testing, generalized device-aware test suites, and platform incubation workflows.
Brian：它提供标准化的硬件接入指南、跨仓库 CI 测试、通用的设备感知测试套件，以及平台孵化流程。

74
00:06:37,088 --> 00:06:41,964
Ava: Cross-repo CI means integration testing across repositories, right?
Ava：跨仓库 CI，就是跨代码仓库做集成测试，对吧？

75
00:06:41,964 --> 00:06:44,206
Why does that matter here?
这在这里为什么重要？

76
00:06:44,206 --> 00:06:45,330
Brian: That's the idea.
Brian：就是这个意思。

77
00:06:45,330 --> 00:06:53,196
Hardware support crosses project boundaries, so a test in just one repository may miss a problem elsewhere.
硬件支持涉及多个项目，只测一个仓库可能漏掉其他地方的问题。

78
00:06:53,196 --> 00:06:57,698
Ava: And device-aware tests check behavior with the device in mind?
Ava：设备感知测试，就是结合设备特性来检查运行行为？

79
00:06:57,698 --> 00:06:58,007
Brian: Yes.
Brian：是的。

80
00:06:58,007 --> 00:07:06,784
The group also described refined device-agnostic APIs and a broader multi-backend test matrix in its roadmap.
工作组的路线图还提到了完善设备无关 API，以及扩大多后端测试矩阵。

81
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:13,901
Ava: Device-agnostic APIs: interfaces intended to work without being tied to one device.
Ava：设备无关 API，就是不绑定某一种设备的接口。

82
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:15,366
Is that fair?
这样理解对吗？

83
00:07:15,366 --> 00:07:21,772
Brian: That's fair. Multi-backend means testing across different hardware implementations.
Brian：对。多后端是指在不同硬件实现上测试。

84
00:07:21,772 --> 00:07:25,364
The article presents this as ongoing work.
文章说这些工作仍在推进。

85
00:07:25,364 --> 00:07:27,278
Ava: Let me check the main point.
Ava：我确认一下重点。

86
00:07:27,278 --> 00:07:32,482
The goal is to make adding hardware less repetitive and easier to test.
目标是减少新增硬件时的重复工作，让测试更容易。

87
00:07:32,482 --> 00:07:32,775
Brian: Yes.
Brian：是的。

88
00:07:32,775 --> 00:07:39,360
And hardware vendors and developers are invited to participate in that working group.
也欢迎硬件厂商和开发者加入这个工作组。

89
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:45,206
Ava: What did the conference say about models and serving? We haven't spent much time there.
Ava：大会对模型和推理服务说了什么？这部分我们还没怎么聊。

90
00:07:45,206 --> 00:07:55,492
Brian: The keynotes ranged from open-weight models to vLLM deployment, distributed acceleration, cloud infrastructure for Qwen, and agent runtimes.
Brian：主题演讲涵盖开放权重模型、vLLM 部署、分布式加速、支持 Qwen 的云基础设施，以及智能体运行时。

91
00:07:55,492 --> 00:07:58,859
Ava: Open-weight means the model weights are available.
Ava：开放权重是指模型权重可供获取。

92
00:07:58,859 --> 00:08:02,778
Did the article give a concrete optimization example?
文章有具体的优化案例吗？

93
00:08:02,778 --> 00:08:03,274
Brian: It did.
Brian：有。

94
00:08:03,274 --> 00:08:13,184
One DeepSeek-R1 case study discussed kernels, routing, parallelism, and serving on the same GB three hundred hardware six months later.
一个 DeepSeek-R1 案例研究讨论了内核、路由、并行和推理服务的优化，以及六个月后在同一款 GB300 硬件上的运行情况。

95
00:08:13,184 --> 00:08:16,678
Ava: Wait, so what changed in the result?
Ava：等等，最终结果有了什么变化？

96
00:08:16,678 --> 00:08:26,268
Brian: In the configuration presented, the optimized system delivered two point seven seven times the throughput and sixty percent lower token cost.
Brian：在演示的配置下，优化后系统的吞吐量达到原来的 2.77 倍，单个 token 的成本降低了 60%。

97
00:08:26,268 --> 00:08:29,775
Ava: Throughput means the amount of work served over time.
Ava：吞吐量就是单位时间内处理的工作量。

98
00:08:29,775 --> 00:08:33,434
Did they separate the effect of each optimization?
他们分别测算了每项优化的效果吗？

99
00:08:33,434 --> 00:08:36,185
Brian: The article doesn't give that breakdown.
Brian：文章没有给出这项细分数据。

100
00:08:36,185 --> 00:08:44,032
It reports the combined result for the presented configuration and cites NVIDIA's twenty twenty-six material.
它报告了所展示配置的综合结果，并引用了 NVIDIA 2026 年的资料。

101
00:08:44,032 --> 00:08:49,350
Ava: So I shouldn't treat those numbers as a promise for my own serving setup.
Ava：所以我不能把这些数字当成自己部署模型服务时的保证。

102
00:08:49,350 --> 00:08:52,618
Brian: Right. They show what that case study achieved.
Brian：对。它们反映的是那个案例取得的结果。

103
00:08:52,618 --> 00:08:58,460
The article doesn't claim every workload or accelerator will see the same gain.
文章没有说所有工作负载或加速器都能获得同样的提升。

104
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:01,066
Ava: Was there another number that stood out?
Ava：还有什么数字值得注意吗？

105
00:09:01,066 --> 00:09:01,344
Brian: Yes.
Brian：是的。

106
00:09:01,344 --> 00:09:09,696
The article cites Mozilla for a change in OpenRouter token traffic served by open-weight models developed in China.
文章引用 Mozilla 的数据，说明中国开发的开放权重模型在 OpenRouter 处理的 token 流量中占比发生了变化。

107
00:09:09,696 --> 00:09:11,798
Ava: How big a change?
Ava：变化有多大？

108
00:09:11,798 --> 00:09:19,276
Brian: Their share rose from two percent in late twenty twenty-four to forty-five percent in April twenty twenty-six.
Brian：占比从 2024 年底的 2% 升至 2026 年 4 月的 45%。

109
00:09:19,276 --> 00:09:24,978
Ava: That's a large shift. But it's specifically OpenRouter traffic, correct?
Ava：变化很大。但这只针对 OpenRouter 的流量，对吗？

110
00:09:24,978 --> 00:09:28,397
Brian: Correct. It isn't a measure of all AI usage.
Brian：对。这不能代表所有 AI 使用情况。

111
00:09:28,397 --> 00:09:35,120
It's one traffic measure that helps explain the conference's focus on open models.
这项流量指标有助于解释大会为何关注开放模型。

112
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:40,390
Ava: What about the keynote from Mark Collier? How does it fit with these examples?
Ava：Mark Collier 的主题演讲呢？它和这些例子有什么关系？

113
00:09:40,390 --> 00:09:51,612
Brian: He focused on an open ecosystem where researchers, developers, model builders, and hardware companies can work together from experimentation to production.
Brian：他强调开放生态，让研究人员、开发者、模型构建者和硬件公司从实验到生产都能协作。

114
00:09:51,612 --> 00:09:57,194
Ava: That sounds broad. Did the conference connect those people in person too?
Ava：范围挺广。大会也让这些人在线下交流了吗？

115
00:09:57,194 --> 00:09:57,474
Brian: Yes.
Brian：是的。

116
00:09:57,474 --> 00:10:06,712
There were technical demonstrations, community meetings, a Foundation press conference, and conversations at the PyTorch booth.
现场有技术演示、社区会议、基金会新闻发布会，以及 PyTorch 展台上的交流。

117
00:10:06,712 --> 00:10:12,366
Ava: And Technical Advisory Council members met in Shanghai on day one.
Ava：技术顾问委员会成员还在大会第一天于上海会面。

118
00:10:12,366 --> 00:10:13,018
Brian: They did.
Brian：没错。

119
00:10:13,018 --> 00:10:20,324
Those meetings fit the same theme: people working on different layers need places to coordinate.
这些会议也呼应了同一主题：负责不同技术层面的人需要有地方协调工作。

120
00:10:20,324 --> 00:10:21,567
Ava: Here's the catch.
Ava：不过，有个问题。

121
00:10:21,567 --> 00:10:28,570
A conference article can show direction, but it can't answer every implementation question.
会议报道能展示方向，却无法回答所有实施问题。

122
00:10:28,570 --> 00:10:29,229
Brian: Exactly.
Brian：正是如此。

123
00:10:29,229 --> 00:10:39,792
It names projects and work areas, but gives limited detail on individual integrations, test outcomes, and benchmark methods.
文章提到了项目和工作方向，但对具体集成、测试结果和基准测试方法介绍有限。

124
00:10:39,792 --> 00:10:42,542
Ava: What should an engineer watch next?
Ava：工程师接下来该关注什么？

125
00:10:42,542 --> 00:10:51,388
Brian: The Accelerator Integration Working Group's device-agnostic APIs and expanded multi-backend testing are concrete areas to follow.
Brian：可以关注加速器集成工作组的设备无关 API，以及扩展中的多后端测试。

126
00:10:51,388 --> 00:10:54,906
Ava: And the article points to another conference?
Ava：文章还提到了另一场大会？

127
00:10:54,906 --> 00:11:06,416
Brian: PyTorch Conference North America is scheduled for San Jose on October twentieth and twenty-first, with maintainers from PyTorch, vLLM, DeepSpeed, and Ray.
Brian：PyTorch 北美大会定于 10 月 20 日至 21 日在圣何塞举行，PyTorch、vLLM、DeepSpeed 和 Ray 的维护者将出席。

128
00:11:06,416 --> 00:11:10,630
Ava: Let's finish with three things to remember. First?
Ava：最后总结三点。第一点？

129
00:11:10,630 --> 00:11:16,836
Brian: First, the open AI stack connects applications all the way down to diverse hardware.
Brian：第一，开放式 AI 技术栈从应用一直延伸到多种硬件。

130
00:11:16,836 --> 00:11:21,959
Ava: Second, hardware integration needs shared guidelines and tests.
Ava：第二，硬件集成需要共同的准则和测试。

131
00:11:21,959 --> 00:11:26,618
Third, promising numbers need their original context.
第三，看待亮眼数据要结合原始背景。

132
00:11:26,618 --> 00:11:31,000
Brian: That's it. Thanks for listening, and we'll see you next time.
Brian：就是这样。感谢收听，我们下次见。
